diff --git a/docs/spec/decisions.md b/docs/spec/decisions.md
index 67b3283f..13de940c 100644
--- a/docs/spec/decisions.md
+++ b/docs/spec/decisions.md
@@ -79,6 +79,7 @@ edits the existing row and notes the supersession (do not delete).
 | D62 | Periodic digest structure is deterministic tag arithmetic; the LLM touches only per-cluster prose | The non-degraded periodic group digest groups its clusters under topic category headers computed by **pure tag arithmetic** — a qualifying-tag threshold (a tag must be carried by at least N clusters, default 3), highest-digest-wide-count assignment with alphabetical tie-break, a fold-back second pass that folds under-threshold categories into Other, section order by assigned-cluster count descending then alphabetical with Other always last, a per-section item cap (default 12) with a localized "+N more" line, and one closing affordance line (`commands.md` §Periodic group digests). No LLM call participates in the structure: given the same clusters + tags the section layout is byte-identical, extending the D19 "deterministic retrieval, LLM only for prose" boundary from the retrieved set to the digest's rendered structure. Per-cluster LLM prose is generated only for the clusters actually shown (capped-out clusters would waste LLM calls on never-rendered prose). The per-cluster prose step, `/summary`'s flat interactive format, and the degraded digest (D17) are unchanged. |
 | D63 | Periodic digest delivery is one message per category; one slot contributes at most one aggregate counter outcome; redelivery may duplicate | A non-degraded periodic digest (D62) with at least one post is delivered as **one outbound message per category** (plus one "Other" message when the Other bucket is non-empty, bounded at categories+1) — never two categories merged into one message, never one category split across two. Category messages are sent **sequentially in section order** (D62 order: assigned-cluster count descending, alphabetical ties, Other last), never fanned out in parallel; sequential order is what makes "closing affordance on the last message" deterministic and preserves the digest's narrative order (a parallel implementation would pass every other listed delivery property). The closing affordance is folded into the LAST section's text inside the render pass, so the per-message affordance lands once per digest on the final category message only. The zero-posts fixed reply and the degraded (D17) headlines-only digest stay single-message — they have no per-category structure. **Partial-failure policy:** each category message runs the existing per-message TRANSIENT-retry / PERMANENT-abort ladder independently; a TRANSIENT failure retries only that message, and a PERMANENT failure on one category still delivers the others (partial success is visible, not all-or-nothing). **Slot-level counter attribution:** one digest slot contributes at most ONE outcome to the per-group consecutive-permanent-failure counter — delivered via the new `OutboundDelivery.deliverSequenceToGroup(adapter, messages, groupId)`, which loops the per-message ladder unattributed (the same form `deliver` uses for DMs) and applies a single aggregate afterwards: any success resets, all-permanent increments once, an interrupt that stops the sequence early attributes nothing. This is not optional hardening: the threshold (3 in every profile except `pi`, 5) was calibrated for one message per slot, and SimpleX classifies a send on a closed or not-yet-started WebSocket as an IMMEDIATE PERMANENT (`SimpleXWebSocketClient.sendCommand`; `onClose` fails every pending future the same way), so naive per-category `deliverToGroup` calls would let one routine simplex-chat subprocess restart during the sequential loop yield ≥3 instant PERMANENTs in milliseconds — soft-removing a healthy group with no admin notification. The threshold's documented "always > 1, so a single misclassified failure cannot trigger it" invariant dies under naive per-message attribution; collapsing the slot to one aggregate preserves it. Each category message carries a per-(slot, category) correlationId `digest-<groupId>-<windowStart>-<categorySlug>`, where `categorySlug` is the category's tag string as-is and the literal `other` for the Other bucket; the id is NOT stable across regenerations and nothing dedups on it (D64). **Redelivery may duplicate:** nothing in v1 records which categories were delivered, so `/retry --digest` re-runs the slot and re-posts every category it produces, including any that already landed — consistent with `commands.md` §Conversation control, which commits that `/retry --digest` posts a new message. Each call is independent and produces a fresh N sends. Gap-filling redelivery — sending only the categories a prior attempt missed — needs persisted per-(slot, category) delivery state AND replay state, and is M1-652's job (decided 2026-07-20, arm (b): M1-652 also persists the section list at render time and replays it on retry); this row records that **M1-642 owns none of it**. Recorded by M1-642. |
 | D64 | Outbound delivery is at-least-once; `correlationId` is not a dedup key and no chokepoint mechanism will make it one | v1 outbound delivery is **at-least-once**: a retry after an ambiguous send failure MAY deliver a duplicate, and nothing suppresses it. `OutboundMessage.correlationId`'s only SPI commitment is **non-nullity** — the prior javadoc claim that it is "stable across retries of the same logical outbound" was **false** and is retired, as is `06-messaging.md` §6.3.5's "adapters SHOULD deduplicate by correlationId over a 60-second window" (no adapter ever did). Stability is a per-call-site property: all but three `new OutboundMessage(...)` sites mint a fresh `UUID.randomUUID()`, so a correlationId-keyed dedup table is inert at nearly every site, and the three stable-id sites (`DigestWorker`, `ApproveGroupCommandHandler`, `RejectGroupCommandHandler`) are deliberate re-sends that must NOT be suppressed — `/retry --digest` is spec-mandated to post again (`commands.md` §Conversation control). The retired 60 s window was also shorter than the sequence it covered: back-off sleeps total under 750 ms, but `SimpleXAdapter.ACK_TIMEOUT` is 30 s per transmit, so three attempts span ~90 s (× N for an N-chunk send). **The bar to reopening this is a transport-level fact, not a Provider-side design:** the duplicate arises from an ambiguous transmit, and only the adapter or transport can observe whether it landed — a Provider chokepoint records success only after the adapter reports it, so it is structurally blind to that case. Any future at-most-once work is therefore adapter/transport-level. This row exists because the false stability claim was load-bearing in two ticket designs (M1-642, M1-652) before anyone checked the call sites; recording the *reason* the guarantee is unavailable is what keeps it from being re-derived. Recorded by M1-653 (pure-doc; no behavior change). |
+| D65 | Gap-filling `/retry --digest` replays persisted render bytes for the categories a prior attempt missed; the cache-row horizon is decoupled from the user-facing cooldown | A `/retry --digest` for a slot whose render persisted its per-category sections (V61 `digest_section`, written by `DigestWorker.executeSlot` at render time alongside the `summary_cache` upsert) replays ONLY the categories with no `digest_category_delivery` row — byte-faithfully, deterministically, and LLM-free: no post re-collection, no re-render, no translation. The replayed bytes are the exact `renderSections()` output the slot originally produced (the M1-642 arm-(b) pin), so a retry delivers the digest it is retrying rather than one that sweeps in posts published since the crash (a previously-unfiled oddity). A slot with no persisted sections (a degraded slot, a zero-post slot, a pre-V61 cache row, or a crash-stranded cache row) falls back to today's full re-run path; the fallback's render budget is bounded by the configured digest window width, never by the retention horizon, so a retry never acquires a many-hours LLM timeout. **Delivery recording rule:** a category message the adapter accepts records a `digest_category_delivery` row on scheduled delivery AND on replay delivery alike — through the same `DigestDelivery.deliver` seam (a delegating adapter wrapper), so a failed send records nothing and the existing per-category TRANSIENT/PERMANENT ladder is unchanged. The recording is idempotent (`ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`) to tolerate the scheduled-vs-replay race D64's at-least-once permits. **Counter-safety guard:** a replay that filters down to zero categories short-circuits BEFORE the chokepoint (`OutboundDelivery.deliverSequenceToGroup`'s empty-list path would increment the permanent-failure counter — the method is frozen, so the caller-side guard is the only in-scope option) and returns a distinct `ALL_ALREADY_DELIVERED` reply. **Horizon decoupling:** `summary_cache.expires_at` and the V61 tables' prune eligibility move from `infochat.digest.retry-cooldown` (default PT2M) to a new `infochat.digest.replay-retention` (default PT24H); the cooldown returns to cooldown-only duty. The decoupling is load-bearing: the replay path needs a horizon wide enough to cover the operator window a human notices a truncated digest (~hours), but the cooldown must stay short to bound LLM cost from rapid retries. Coupling them — the pre-M1-652 shape — meant widening the horizon widened the cooldown, so the ~2-minute cliff was unreachable. The fallback slot's `windowEnd` is clamped to `min(cache.expires_at, now + window_width)` so the clamp preserves today's expired-row-degrades semantics while still killing the cliff for live rows. **Persistence ordering:** sections persist BEFORE any category message leaves the process (after the cache upsert, before the adapter lookup) so a crash between render and deliver leaves the bytes durably readable for a later gap-filling retry — the crash-window correctness property the whole design rests on. Landed by M1-652 (V61 + the replay/delivery path). |
 | D66 | The command-intent corpus is the second embedded corpus; retrieval is match-not-assert, tier-filter-before-return, and explicitly silent on chat delivery | The `doc_embedding` table (V60) is the second embedded corpus alongside `post_embedding` (V11) — one Postgres, one pgvector, one embedding model, both corpora at the 768 dimension pinned by `embedding_metadata`'s singleton (D54). The shape is the structural opposite of `post_embedding` on every load-bearing axis: NOT partitioned (a command-intent document has no TTL — it is correct until the command itself changes), Provider-written (the `CommandIntentIndexBuilder` runs at Provider startup and is the sole writer), and grant-opened to provider INSERT + DELETE so the DELETE-then-INSERT upsert can run (UPDATE withheld; the narrow-grant posture the M1-648 r2 CLEAN audit verified). Three invariants govern the read path (the `helpLookup` chat tool): (1) **Match-not-assert** — embedded text is used only for MATCHING, never for ASSERTING. The tool returns the matched command NAME plus the catalogue's one-line short-help line, composed at call time from the runtime `HelpCommandHandler.CATALOGUE`'s `bundleKey`; full usage/example bodies never enter the model context (the second out-of-scope entry of M1-664). A stale intent row can degrade a match but can never produce wrong syntax, because the tool never reads the stored text. (2) **Tier-filter-before-return** — the caller's visible command-name set is bound as `target_ref = ANY(?)` INSIDE the WHERE of the pgvector query, so an invisible command's name never enters the model context. This is the M1-647 existence-oracle defense widened to free-text input (a wider attack surface than the slash-command unknown-name path). The visible set is computed by the SAME `HelpCommandHandler.visible` tier predicate `/help` applies at listing time, resolved for the chat-tool identity via `HelpCommandHandler.resolveCallerTier(userId, scopeKind, scopeId)`. (3) **D19 determinism** — the match is decided entirely by SQL (pgvector cosine + threshold + tier filter), reproducible on unchanged DB state; the LLM never picks the match. This row is **explicitly silent on chat delivery**: how a matched command's usage reaches the user is the M1-663 spec amendment, implemented by M1-665. The M1-648 r2 redteam regression (medium INJECTION: post-sanitize, model-elected append of privileged command usage) is what the silence buys — the delivery decision is made deliberately elsewhere, not inherited here. Companion to M1-647 (the probation-reachable synonym path, NOT superseded by this corpus — both tiers ship). Recorded by M1-664. |
 | D67 | Chat-mode command usage is delivered by a deterministic trigger over the caller's inbound text, composed via the `/help` runtime path, capped at one block per reply | When a chat-mode caller asks in free text how to do something a command provides ("how do I stop seeing posts from this source" → `/unfollow-source`), the usage block is delivered by **deterministic code**, not by the model electing to deliver it. The trigger lives in `ChatAgent.doHandle` ahead of the LLM call: it embeds the caller's inbound text and probes `doc_embedding` (the M1-664 command-intent corpus) via the SAME `CommandIntentIndex.lookupCommand` entry point the `helpLookup` tool uses, with the caller's visible command set bound as `target_ref = ANY(?)` INSIDE the WHERE (tier-filter-before-return, inherited from D66). A model-elected `helpLookup` call reaches the model context only — never the delivery decision. The trigger's similarity threshold (0.70) is intentionally higher than the tool's (0.60): the tool answers the model's question "which command matches this phrase"; the trigger answers "did the CALLER ask how to do something" — a different question with a higher false-positive cost (an unsolicited usage block on every turn that merely mentions a topic). Both thresholds are pinned as code constants; recalibration is a follow-up. The composed block is the byte-identical output of `HelpCommandHandler.composeUsageBlock` — the same method `/help <cmd>` invokes — plus a fixed bundle-localized header (`CHAT_HELP_DELIVERY_HEADER`). It is appended AFTER sanitize + translate and is the SINGLE authorized post-sanitize accretion under the M1-663 amendment to security.md §LLM output sanitizer: both the emission DECISION (deterministic SQL) and the composed BYTES (fixed bundle keys + closed-list CATALOGUE walk) are deterministic end-to-end. The block is bundle-localized per the scope's `/lang` (the `/help` path's existing behavior), so it does NOT pass through `TranslationPipeline` (D43 two-path rule). At most ONE block per reply: the trigger's SQL is `LIMIT 1`, so at most one match drives at most one composeUsageBlock call. Defense-in-depth: `composeUsageBlock` re-checks tier visibility before composing (a match the SQL tier filter should already have excluded is caught here too — `adminUsageNeverDeliveredToNonAdmin`). The M1-648 r2 INJECTION regression (post-sanitize, model-elected append of privileged command usage) is structurally dead under this design: an attacker-influenced model cannot cause delivery, because the model is never asked and its tool elections do not flow into the trigger. Recorded by M1-665. |
 
diff --git a/infochat-core/src/main/resources/db/migration/V61__digest_replay_state.sql b/infochat-core/src/main/resources/db/migration/V61__digest_replay_state.sql
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..80361140
--- /dev/null
+++ b/infochat-core/src/main/resources/db/migration/V61__digest_replay_state.sql
@@ -0,0 +1,113 @@
+-- V61: digest_replay_state — persisted render output + per-category delivery
+-- records (M1-652).
+--
+-- Lands the two tables the gap-filling /retry --digest path needs so a
+-- mid-sequence Provider death stops silently losing undelivered digest
+-- categories. arm (b) of the M1-652 fork (user decision 2026-07-20): the
+-- rendered section list is PERSISTED at render time as the exact delivery
+-- bytes, and /retry --digest replays only the categories with no delivery
+-- record — deterministic, byte-faithful, LLM-free. A full re-run fallback
+-- covers section-less slots (pre-V61 rows, degraded slots, zero-post slots,
+-- crash-stranded cache rows); see docs/spec/commands.md §Conversation control
+-- and decision D65.
+--
+-- Both tables are keyed by (group_id, window_start, category_slug) — the SAME
+-- tuple DigestDelivery composes the per-(slot, category) correlationId from
+-- (DigestDelivery.java:82), so the persisted delivery-state key and the
+-- outbound correlationId cannot drift. window_start joins summary_cache.
+-- slot_fired_at exactly (the cache upsert keys on windowStart ==
+-- DigestSlot.windowStart()); slot_kind is omitted because morning/evening
+-- slots use distinct center hours (DigestScheduler), so (group_id,
+-- window_start) cannot collide across slot kinds — the pinned PKs are safe.
+--
+-- Atomic Flyway migration: both CREATE TABLEs + grants apply in one
+-- transaction so a partial failure rolls back cleanly.
+
+-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
+-- digest_section — the persisted render output, one row per section in
+-- renderSections() order. Written alongside the summary_cache upsert at
+-- render time (DigestWorker.executeSlot, after renderSections() returns
+-- and before per-category delivery) so a crash between render and deliver
+-- leaves the sections durably readable for replay. Deleted-then-inserted
+-- atomically per slot (a slot's prior sections are wiped before the new
+-- list is written, in one transaction with the matching delivery-record
+-- wipe — see DigestSectionRepository.replaceSlotSections — so replay never
+-- half-applies when a regeneration supersedes an existing row).
+--
+-- group_id        Internal groups.id; joins summary_cache.group_id.
+-- window_start    DigestSlot.windowStart(); joins summary_cache.slot_fired_at.
+-- category_slug   The section's tag string as-is, literal "other" for the
+--                 null (Other) bucket — identical to DigestDelivery.java:81.
+-- position        0-based list index of the section in renderSections()
+--                 output; drives replay's position-order traversal.
+-- content         The EXACT delivery bytes — RenderedSection.text(),
+--                 affordance folded into the last section and flag-on
+--                 roll-up prefixes inside their sections (M1-642 arm-(b) pin).
+-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+CREATE TABLE digest_section (
+    group_id       UUID NOT NULL,
+    window_start   TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
+    category_slug  TEXT NOT NULL,
+    position       INT NOT NULL,
+    content        TEXT NOT NULL,
+    PRIMARY KEY (group_id, window_start, category_slug)
+);
+
+-- position-order traversal of a slot's sections. The PK already covers
+-- (group_id, window_start) lookups via the prefix, but a position-ordered
+-- read would otherwise need a sort; this secondary index keeps the replay
+-- path a single ordered scan.
+CREATE INDEX idx_digest_section_slot_order
+    ON digest_section (group_id, window_start, position);
+
+-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
+-- digest_category_delivery — the per-(slot, category) delivery record.
+-- A row exists iff the adapter accepted that category's message (recorded
+-- by the delegating MessagingAdapter wrapper inside DigestDelivery on a
+-- normal return from send(); a failed send records nothing, so the existing
+-- per-category TRANSIENT/PERMANENT ladder is unchanged). /retry --digest
+-- reads this to compute which categories still need sending and skips the
+-- rest — the gap-fill.
+--
+-- delivered_at is a record-only write (it logs WHEN the adapter accepted,
+-- it gates nothing), so it stays on SQL now() rather than the injected
+-- java.time.Clock the replay decision gates use (CLAUDE.md §Injectable
+-- time in decision logic — the display/record exemption).
+-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+CREATE TABLE digest_category_delivery (
+    group_id       UUID NOT NULL,
+    window_start   TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
+    category_slug  TEXT NOT NULL,
+    delivered_at   TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
+    PRIMARY KEY (group_id, window_start, category_slug)
+);
+
+-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
+-- Per-table GRANTs (aligned with docs/spec/security.md §DB roles, V60's
+-- narrow-grant posture the M1-648 r2 CLEAN audit verified).
+--
+-- Both tables are Provider-owned: DigestWorker writes sections at render
+-- time, DigestDelivery records deliveries on adapter acceptance, and the
+-- retry path reads both. The replace shape is DELETE-then-INSERT (one
+-- transaction), the delivery record is idempotent INSERT ON CONFLICT DO
+-- NOTHING, and pruning is a prefix DELETE — so INSERT + DELETE + SELECT
+-- is the full surface. UPDATE is withheld and REVOKEd: nothing in the
+-- Provider ever needs to mutate a row in place (every change is a full
+-- slot-replace or a no-op-conflict insert), and withholding UPDATE keeps
+-- the grant surface minimal in case a role-inheritance surprise widens
+-- the defaults.
+--
+-- The Collector is granted NOTHING on either table. Replay state is
+-- Provider-only; the Collector's summary_cache grants (V23/V46) are
+-- unchanged.
+-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+GRANT SELECT, INSERT, DELETE ON digest_section TO infochat_provider;
+REVOKE UPDATE ON digest_section FROM infochat_provider;
+REVOKE UPDATE ON digest_section FROM PUBLIC;
+
+GRANT SELECT, INSERT, DELETE ON digest_category_delivery TO infochat_provider;
+REVOKE UPDATE ON digest_category_delivery FROM infochat_provider;
+REVOKE UPDATE ON digest_category_delivery FROM PUBLIC;
